Task 4: End-to-End Rollout Sequence
Combine your domain/OU design, DNS/DHCP configuration, and file share provisioning into a
single sequenced deployment. (Integrative — multi-step flow combining 3+ concepts.)
Sequence all commands from Tasks 1–3 in the order they must actually run, with a one-line
reason for each position in the sequence.
Add a step that joins a client machine to the domain and verifies it received an IP address
from your Task 2 scope.
Add a step that configures a VPN endpoint (SSTP or IKEv2) so a remote user can reach the Task
3 file share.
Summarize the full rollout as a deployment checklist (6–10 steps).
==># 1. Promote the server to a domain controller (must exist before anything else can join or be created in AD)
Install-ADDSForest -DomainName "corp.company.local" -DomainNetbiosName "CORP" `
    -ForestMode WinThreshold -DomainMode WinThreshold -InstallDns:$true `
    -SafeModeAdministratorPassword (ConvertTo-SecureString "P@ssw0rd!2026" -AsPlainText -Force) `
    -Force:$true

# 2. Create the AD-integrated DNS zone (requires the forest/domain to exist first)
Add-DnsServerPrimaryZone -Name "corp.company.local" -ReplicationScope "Domain" -DynamicUpdate "Secure"

# 3. Create the DHCP scope (clients need addresses before they can reach the DC/DNS at all)
Add-DhcpServerv4Scope -Name "Corp-Main-Scope" -StartRange 192.168.10.10 -EndRange 192.168.10.250 `
    -SubnetMask 255.255.255.0 -State Active
Add-DhcpServerv4ExclusionRange -ScopeId 192.168.10.0 -StartRange 192.168.10.10 -EndRange 192.168.10.20

# 4. Configure DHCP failover (protects the scope created in step 3; requires the scope to exist first)
Add-DhcpServerv4Failover -Name "Corp-DHCP-Failover" -ScopeId 192.168.10.0 `
    -PartnerServer "DHCP02.corp.company.local" -Mode HotStandby -ServerRole Active `
    -SharedSecret "FailoverSecret2026!" -MaxClientLeadTime 00:01:00

# 5. Create the file share (needs the DC/domain up so domain groups like CORP\Finance_Group can be referenced)
New-SmbShare -Name "Finance" -Path "D:\Shares\Finance" -FullAccess "CORP\Domain Admins" `
    -ChangeAccess "CORP\Authenticated Users"

# 6. Apply NTFS permissions (must come after the share/folder exists, to lock down actual file-system access)
icacls "D:\Shares\Finance" /inheritance:r
icacls "D:\Shares\Finance" /grant "CORP\Finance_Group:(OI)(CI)M"
icacls "D:\Shares\Finance" /grant "CORP\Domain Admins:(OI)(CI)F"

# 7. Apply FSRM quota and file screen (protects the share/folder just created and permissioned)
New-FsrmQuota -Path "D:\Shares\Finance" -Size 50GB -Description "Finance hard quota"
New-FsrmFileGroup -Name "Ransomware-Blocked-Extensions" -IncludePattern @("*.exe","*.js")
New-FsrmFileScreen -Path "D:\Shares\Finance" -IncludeGroup "Ransomware-Blocked-Extensions" -Active

# 8. Join a client to the domain (domain/DNS/DHCP must all be functional first so the client can locate the DC, get an IP, and register in DNS)
Add-Computer -DomainName "corp.company.local" -Credential "CORP\Administrator" -Restart

# 9. Verify the client received an address from the Task 2 scope
Test-Connection -ComputerName "NewLaptop01" | Select-Object IPV4Address
Get-DhcpServerv4Lease -ScopeId 192.168.10.0 | Where-Object {$_.HostName -like "NewLaptop01*"}

# 10. Configure the VPN endpoint (SSTP, since it tunnels over HTTPS — for remote access to the Finance share)
Install-RemoteAccess -VpnType VpnS2S
Set-VpnServerConfiguration -TunnelType Sstp -SslCertificate "CN=vpn.company.local"


